    "case_id": "57",
    "case_summary": "An 8.5-year-old girl presented with swelling in both knees that had persisted for two years. She reported no issues in other joints. During her initial examination, her growth was normal. Bilateral knee effusions were observed, while other joints and systems appeared normal. Initial laboratory tests showed normal complete blood count (CBC), urine analysis, complement levels, and immunoglobulin levels. ANA was negative. She was started on naproxen and monitored every three months. Over the two-year follow-up period, she did not exhibit symptoms such as hair loss, facial rash, or mouth ulcers. At 10.5 years old, she experienced a recurrence of knee arthritis and nosebleeds.\n\nMRI of both knees with gadolinium demonstrated chronic inflammation with synovial thickening. ANA was positive (1/1280, speckled pattern), anti-dsDNA level was 1/20 positive, C3 (75.7 mg/dl) and C4 (5.4 mg/dl) levels were low.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
